  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Find the area of the region bounded by the four lines whose equations are:\n$$x+y=1,\\quad x-y=1,\\quad -x+y=1,\\quad -x-y=1.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$2$ square units.\n\nThe region consists of four right-angled triangles, each with base and height $1$.",
      "worked": "Setting $y=0$ and $x=0$ gives the axial intercepts:\n\n$x+y=1$: $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$.\n\n$x-y=1$: $(1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$.\n\n$-x+y=1$: $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1)$.\n\n$-x-y=1$: $(-1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$.\n\nJoining the intercepts gives a square with vertices $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$. The axes divide it into four right-angled triangles, each with base and height $1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=4\\times\\frac12\\times1\\times1\\\\A&=2\\text{ square units}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 84
  },
